--- context (Desktop 2 / Phoenix, 2026-09-06) ---
Built to fill the gap in the achieved-TOPS saturation curve between yolov8m
(40.6 GMACs -> 33.1%) and yolov8l (84.3 GMACs -> 39.3%): yolov8s re-exported
at 1280x1280 (models/yolov8s_r1280_cut_xint8.onnx, quantized plain XINT8,
--limit 4 calibration, ~0.77 GB cached per image -- 3.09 GB total, far below
the yolov8x@1280 RAM-wall incident) profiles to 59.66 GMACs/inference
(onnx-tool), landing at 37.5 fps combined @ N=4 -> 4.475 TOPS -> 28.0% of the
16 TOPS nameplate.

The tool's own cross-talk heuristic flagged every single call as a mismatch,
even at N=1 where no concurrency is possible -- this is NOT cross-talk, and
was confirmed as such with the cheapest available check: worker rank 0
always gets the "expect negative" image (img_b) per multi_partition_bench.py's
own assignment (`is_neg = (i == 0)`), and CPU-EP sessions built directly on
both the FP32 cut model (models/yolov8s_r1280_cut.onnx) and this XINT8 model
were compared on the same two images:
  - FP32 CPU: img_a finds class 0 (True, correct), img_b does not (False,
    correct) -- the oracle images and letterbox/decode path are valid at
    this resolution.
  - XINT8 CPU: img_a still finds class 0 (True, correct), but img_b now
    ALSO finds class 0 (True -- a false positive) reproducibly on CPU with
    the identical quantized weights, no NPU or concurrency involved.
This is a genuine --limit 4 under-calibration accuracy defect specific to
this (narrower, shallower) architecture at this resolution, not a hardware
fault: node schedule and MAC count are untouched by Q/DQ scale values, so
the fps/TOPS number above is still valid for the throughput question this
probe exists to answer, but this model has a directly measured (not merely
"unmeasured") accuracy defect and must never be cited for mAP or detection
correctness. Minor tooling note: multi_partition_bench.py's cross-talk
check conflates a quantization-accuracy failure that reproduces solo (N=1)
with real cross-talk; a future version should gate on the N=1 case cleanly
before attributing anything to concurrency.

Surprising result: 59.66 GMACs (between yolov8m's 40.6 and yolov8l's 84.3)
achieves 28.0%, LOWER than yolov8m's 33.1% -- not a monotonic fill of the
gap. Raw MACs/call alone does not predict achieved %; see
results/multi_partition_yolov8s.log (yolov8s at native 640, same weights
architecture, no resolution change) for the follow-up that isolates
resolution from architecture.
